 in reconciliation if it doesn't  happen like that  i do see in 50 years  we're going to hand this off to the next  generation to figure out it's like  for instance  if he was driving a bus for 26 people  kids in there and crash  that all the kids die and he survives it  oh i'm sorry  that doesn't mean anything  for bernard jack the residential school  system changed his life  it destroyed the lives of many around  him and continues to impact his children  and grandchildren's lives today  generational trauma can extend hurt  through families for decades  jack says a papal apology alone is not  justice in his mind  but every step towards the truth  that's at least a good place to begin  heather yorks west global news calgary  ahead fixing your finances tips for  making resolutions and keeping yourself  on track  you're watching global national  it's the season for new year's  resolutions and according to ipsos  polling exclusively for global news  nearly half of us plan to make a big  effort to do things differently in 2022  many of those new year's goals are about  money and gaviola has more on how to  stick to your finance related  resolutions  whether it's getting into better shape  or choosing healthier options the new  year may seem like the perfect time to  commit to new goals in the new year  you're able to  have a fresh perspective according to  ipsos polling for global news 41 said  they plan to make new year's resolutions  of those people 48 aim to pay off debt  45 want to make and stick to a budget 36  percent want to save for a rainy day and  34 say they want to save money for  retirement and invest in 2022.  you've heard of dry january right well  instead of cutting out booze personal  finance author melissa leong plans to  spend only on necessities for at least  21 days in january her advice for  sticking to a money-related resolution  be clear about why you're saving for  example people who  put money into envelopes labeled with  their children's names were less likely  to dip into those envelopes because it  had a greater meaning for them and if  you can automate savings so the money  comes right out of your account then  you're more likely to stick to your  goals a lot of people put savings at the  bottom of their personal finance plan so  they spend on their bills first then  they spend on their wants next and then  they you know save whatever's left over  flip that around start saving first when  it comes to deciding what your targets  are be specific and realistic you can  say to yourself by december 31  2022 i would just save three thousand  dollars and i will do so by setting  aside money  every single paycheck  it's just as simple as that rather than  saying i want to save start building  those habits that might last throughout  then the year in a month's time so every  time you want to make a purchase say for  example more than a hundred dollars say  i'm gonna spend 24 hours thinking about  this purchase before i actually make it  once you're clear on your goals find an  accountability partner and schedule  check-ins to keep you on track if you  don't feel comfortable choosing a friend  or family member you can find an online  community just for this studies show  that when you have an accountability  partner people with peer support  actually save more money even if that  conversation was through text than those  who did not one final piece of advice  even if you follow all of these steps  life happens and you could get derailed  go easy on yourself if your 2022 plans  are delayed and become part of your  financial resolutions for 2023 ann  gaviola global news toronto  next therapy through art how the  pandemic helped uncover a new passion  for a toronto boy with epilepsy  we don't have to remind you how  difficult and dark these last few years  have felt but if you've thrown yourself  into a creative project during the  pandemic you know how powerful it can be  karen lieberman has the story of how  painting has changed the life of one  toronto boy  blue and orange and yellow angry  alessandro rizzotti wants to be an  artist when he grows up his mother says  the fact that he can even articulate  that is a miracle  good job we always say he's a warrior  and a fighter alessandro is only seven  but his medical history is long he  survived neonatal meningitis followed by  a stroke and he's had two brain  surgeries he also had infantile spasm  which is a very rare type of seizures  from there  he developed epilepsy  and right now he's dealing with adhd  and global delay  doctors weren't sure alessandro would  ever walk or talk  that's how you do that i'm so proud of  him he came a long way  uh you know he still takes therapies and  um and i went to the lego show okay  you see the gold that's kind of like  so you see venus and you see the earth  and most mars is there  he has plenty to say mostly about his  art  neptune when the pandemic began life in  lockdown was difficult for this family  when we were stuck at home it was very  hard  so you know i had to think of things and  and then school on top of that we were  doing school from home painting became  alessandro's passion  i'm gonna be on tv i think uh we found  his hidden talent he actually sold few  as a request i i shipped few paintings  to us to new york and texas  his latest creation a painting to be  auctioned off for sickkids hospital a  way to give back alessandro also  fundraises for epilepsy toronto it means  that we can continue to give the support  that we gave to that family to many  other families and that's and many other  people one in 100 people live with  epilepsy that's over 40 000 people in  the toronto area for alessandro creating  unique artwork is therapeutic  karen lieberman global news toronto  beautiful alessandro and that is global  national for this tuesday i'm colleen  christie tonight's your canada is this  festive scene at butchart gardens in  brentwood bay bc a place i know well  there are beautiful spots all over  canada please email us yours to viewers  at globalnational.com  thanks for watching and i hope to see  you here again tomorrow take care of  yourselves good night  [Music]
 

